{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense collapses because it relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as an objective metric to justify preferring better-corroborated theories, yet explicitly acknowledges in its own counterconditions that Popper later rejected verisimilitude as a formalizable concept. If the metric used to define 'better' is logically invalid or unformalizable within Popper's own system (as admitted by Tichý and Miller in the 1970s), then the preference cannot be grounded in 'verisimilitude' without smuggling in an unjustified metaphysical assumption. The target attempts to have it both ways: using verisimilitude to solve the problem while noting its failure, thereby failing to provide a defensible basis for preference strictly on Popper's terms without reintroducing induction or relying on a broken concept.","premise":"Popper's later rejection of verisimilitude as a formalizable concept invalidates the metric used to define 'better'.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot be formally defined, what non-inductive logical structure allows a scientist to rationally prefer one unfalsified theory over another without assuming the future will resemble the past?","target":"a0cfc5ef347aeb4d66c75fd1c9552e368157c7ee5577ff774160a2c7abb15fea"}]}